   21	/**
   22	 * Answers whether this account can legally queue a unit right now, and which one.
   23	 *
   24	 * Roles are taken in the order a miner's opening takes them: cargo first, because every later
   25	 * fleet action needs somewhere to put loot and resources, then the colony ship that unlocks a
   26	 * second planet, which is what fleetsave, transport and scouting all wait on. The two intel-driven
   27	 * roles come after the opening, because each waits on an observation: defence when the host says a
   28	 * hostile is inbound or when the planet has something the need evaluator says is worth a wall, and
   29	 * a combat escort when a fresh report shows a defended target the account's own fleet is not
   30	 * expected to crack.
   31	 *
   32	 * Power is the one role the building queue shares: a planet that is short and whose capacity the
   33	 * building queue cannot take buys it from the yard instead, which is what a player does when the
   34	 * plant is out of reach or the queue is busy. It is offered only when the building planner's own
   35	 * gate says no, so the two never compete over the same shortfall.
   36	 *
   37	 * Which object fills each role is read from the host: cargo is the ship with the largest cargo
   38	 * capacity per metal-equivalent cost the planet can actually queue, combat is the hull with the best
   39	 * attack per metal-equivalent cost, the colony ship is the ship the host's own colonisation mission
   40	 * consumes, and defence is the component `DefenseCompositionPlanner` says this account's doctrine is
   41	 * most behind on. A mod-added hull with a better ratio becomes its role's unit with no edit here.
   42	 *
   43	 * The standing wall pass below is the one place the account's whole planet list is weighed at once,
   44	 * and it is the only role that is collected before it is chosen: the account's wall spreads across
   45	 * its planets one login at a time, each login serving the first planet still standing at zero.
   46	 *
   47	 * A bare planet whose yard cannot yet take any defence unit is reached by the building planner's
   48	 * defence-prerequisite step, not here: a wall waits on the shipyard that builds it. A planet that
   49	 * holds an order in the yard is served, whether or not the host has finished building it.
   50	 *
   51	 * The pass's plan is marked, because where the order is placed decides whether it happens: the
   52	 * session's building steps are executed first and spend the balance the wall was priced against, so
   53	 * the host refused the wall order and the planet stayed bare on every login (QUAL-003). The marker
   54	 * lets the schedule run the login's first wall ahead of those steps.
   55	 */
   56	class QueueableUnitPlanner
   57	{
   58	    private const CRYSTAL_WEIGHT = 1.5;
   59	
   60	    private const DEUTERIUM_WEIGHT = 2.0;
   61	
   62	    /** The first cargo batch is one hull: sizing for a raid payload arrives with the raid itself. */
   63	    private const FIRST_CARGO_AMOUNT = 1;
   64	
   65	    /** The same 24 h staleness window the observation publishes target reports with. */
   66	    private const INTEL_TTL_HOURS = 24;
